====Verb====
{{en-verb|wears|wearing|wore|past2=ware|past2_qual=obsolete|worn|past_ptc2=wore|past_ptc3=worne|past_ptc2_qual=now colloquial and nonstandard|past_ptc3_qual=obsolete}}

# {{senseid|en|to have on}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[have on]]:
## To [[carry]] or have [[equipped]] on or about one's body, as an item of [[clothing]], [[equipment]], [[decoration]], etc.
##: {{ux|en|He's '''wearing''' some nice pants today.&emsp; She '''wore''' her medals with pride.&emsp; Please '''wear''' your seatbelt.&emsp; Can you '''wear''' makeup and sunscreen at {{nowrap|the same time?&emsp;}} {{nowrap|He was}} '''wearing''' his lunch after tripping and falling {{nowrap|into the buffet.}}}}
##* {{RQ:Weyman Chippinge Borough|I|01|passage=It was April 22, 1831, and a young man was walking down Whitehall in the direction of Parliament Street. He '''wore''' shepherd's plaid trousers and the swallow-tail coat of the day, with a figured muslin cravat wound about his wide-spread collar.}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=5|passage=‘It's rather like a beautiful Inverness cloak one has inherited. Much too good to hide away, so one '''wears''' it instead of an overcoat and pretends it's an amusing new fashion.’}}
## To have or carry on one's person [[habitually]], [[consistently]]; or, to maintain in a particular [[fashion]] or [[manner]].
##: {{ux|en|He '''wears''' eyeglasses.&emsp; She '''wears''' her hair in braids.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=It was a joy to snatch some brief respite, and find himself in the rectory drawing–room. Listening here was as pleasant as talking; just to watch was pleasant. The young priests who lived here '''wore''' cassocks and birettas; their faces were fine and mild, yet really strong, like the rector's face; and in their intercourse with him and his wife they seemed to be brothers.}}
## To [[bear]] or [[display]] in one's [[aspect]] or [[appearance]].
##: {{ux|en|She '''wore''' a smile all day.&emsp; He walked out of the courtroom '''wearing''' an air of satisfaction.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1870|author=Marion Harland|title=Helen Gardner's Wedding-day|page=139
|passage=Then the bridegroom came slowly up the walk, '''wearing''' a very unbridegroomlike aspect, {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|to erode}} To [[erode]]:
## To [[eat away]] at, [[erode]], [[diminish]], or [[consume]] [[gradually]]; to cause a gradual [[deterioration]] in; to produce (some change) through [[attrition]], [[exposure]], or constant use.
##: {{ux|en|You're going to '''wear''' a hole in the bottom of those shoes.&emsp; The water has slowly '''worn''' a channel into these rocks.&emsp; Long illness had '''worn''' the bloom from her cheeks.&emsp; Exile had '''worn''' the man {{nowrap|to a shadow.}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dunsany Pegana
|passage=And They made the Moon, with his face wrinkled with many mountains and '''worn''' with a thousand valleys, to regard with pale eyes the games of the small gods, and to watch throughout the resting time of Māna-Yood-Sushāī; to watch, to regard all things, and be silent.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To undergo gradual deterioration; become [[impaired]]; be [[reduced]] or consumed gradually due to any continued [[process]], [[activity]], or [[use]].
##: {{ux|en|The tiles were '''wearing''' thin due to years of children's feet.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|passage=His stock of money{{...}} began to '''wear''' very low.}}
##* {{RQ:Disraeli Endymion
|passage=The family that had raised it '''wore''' out in the earlier part of this century}}
## {{senseid|en|to exhaust, fatigue, expend, or weary}} To [[exhaust]], [[fatigue]], [[expend]], or [[weary]].
##: {{ux|en|His neverending criticism has finally '''worn''' my patience.&emsp; Toil and care soon '''wear''' the spirit.&emsp; Our physical advantage allowed us to '''wear''' the other team out and win.}}
##* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=vii|page=41|passage=Our ſoules, whoſe faculties can comprehend<br>The wondrous Architecture of the world:<br>And meaſure euery wandring planets courſe,<br>Still climing after knowledge infinite,<br>And alwaies mouing as the reſtles ſpheares,<br>Wils vs to '''weare''' our ſelues & neuer reſt, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} (in the phrase "''wearing on (someone)''") To cause [[annoyance]], [[irritation]], [[fatigue]], or [[weariness]] near the point of an exhaustion of patience.
##: {{ux|en|Her high pitched voice is really '''wearing''' on me lately.}}
# {{senseid|en|to endure}} To [[endure]]:
## {{lb|en|colloquial|with "it"}} To overcome one's reluctance and [[endure]] a (previously specified) situation.
##: {{ux|en|I know you don't like working with him, but you'll just have to '''wear''' it.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[last]] or remain [[durable]] under hard use or over time; to retain [[usefulness]], [[value]], or desirable qualities under any continued [[strain]] or long period of time; sometimes said of a person, regarding the quality of being easy or difficult to [[tolerate]].
##: {{ux|en|Don't worry, this fabric will '''wear'''. These pants will last you for years.&emsp; This color '''wears''' so well. I must have washed this sweater a thousand times.&emsp; I have to say, our friendship has '''worn''' pretty well.&emsp; It's hard to get to know him, {{nowrap|but he '''wears''' well.}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|of time}} To pass [[slowly]], [[gradually]] or [[tediously]].
##: {{ux|en|'''wear''' on'', '''''wear''' away.&emsp; As the years '''wore''' on, we seemed to have less and less {{nowrap|in common.}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|act=V|scene=i|passage=Away, I say; time '''wears'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=2|passage=Thus '''wore''' out night.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|VI||That loss is common would not make<br>⁠My own less bitter, rather more:<br>⁠Too common! Never morning '''wore'''<br>To evening, but some heart did break.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To bring (a [[sailing]] [[vessel]]) onto the other [[tack]] by bringing the [[wind]] around the [[stern]] (as opposed to [[tacking]] when the wind is brought around the [[bow]]); to come round on another tack by turning away from the wind.
#: {{synonyms|en|gybe}}

